# R7 (reports/gates.md, "Post-B16 remediation gates"): make README's
# byte-identity claim survive a cross-platform check.
#
# README says "two runs of the same seeds produce byte-identical output --
# the artifact carries no wall-clock timing, precisely so that the claim can
# be checked by hashing and not only by reading numbers." That was true on
# one machine and false across two: every artifact writer called
# `write_text(..., encoding="utf-8")` with no `newline=`, so Python's
# universal-newline translation emitted CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux, and
# there was no `.gitattributes` to normalise what git stored either. Same
# seeds, same code, two different hashes.
#
# Two halves, and both are needed:
#   1. every artifact writer now passes `newline="\n"` explicitly (eval/run.py,
#      eval/report.py, eval/offramp_channel.py, eval/ltv_sensitivity.py,
#      eval/export_mandates.py, eval/golden_check.py, eval/sim2.py,
#      eval/design_matrix_comparison.py, scripts/checkpoint.py,
#      scripts/dashboard_data.py, src/execute/shadow.py);
#   2. this file, so git stores LF regardless of the checkout platform and a
#      diff between a Windows and a Linux run is a diff in the NUMBERS.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` covers files git detects as text. The explicit rows
# below are the ones the claim actually rests on, stated rather than left to
# detection -- a JSON artifact misdetected as binary would silently opt out
# of the guarantee this file exists to provide.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# PowerShell and cmd stay CRLF. These are Windows-only entry points; a
# .cmd file with LF line endings is a real hazard (cmd.exe's parser has
# historically mis-handled them), and nothing about the byte-identity claim
# involves them.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary, never normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
